<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Buto</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tell el-Fara'in, Pe and Dep</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Buto was an ancient city in the north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, at modern Tell el-Fara'in. Formed from the twin settlements of Pe and Dep, it was in later tradition the capital of the predynastic kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> and the seat of the cobra goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadjet">Wadjet</a>. Excavations have revealed settlement layers of the 4th millennium BCE that gave their name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buto-maadi-culture">Buto-Maadi culture</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/buto.jpg" alt="Buto" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Faris knight · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_of_mudbrick_buildings_on_the_northern_mound_of_Buto-Desouk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Buto lay in the north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, about 15 km east of the Rosetta branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and 30 km south of today's coastline. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> called it Per-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadjet">Wadjet</a>, House of Wadjet, and its present name Tell el-Fara'in means Hill of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaohs</a>. The site is a mound of about one square kilometre, with two settlement mounds up to 20 m high, Kom A in the north and Kom C in the south, and the low-lying temple area, Kom B. Wadjet, the cobra goddess, was the patron deity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> and was paired with Nekhbet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> as the Two Ladies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest settlement dates from the middle to late fourth millennium BCE and belongs to the Buto-Ma'adi culture, the most important Lower Egyptian prehistoric culture of the period. Its houses were of wattle and daub, and its pottery and small amounts of imported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> show influence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p><p> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>'s Naqada culture then replaced it, perhaps after a conquest, although Thomas von der Way reads the change as cultural assimilation around 3300 to 3200 BCE. From the start of the 1st Dynasty simple rooms grew into walled complexes at least 50 m wide. Ulrich Hartung interprets a maze-like building of the late 1st Dynasty as an Early Dynastic palace, which a fire devastated around the middle of the 2nd Dynasty.</p><p>No settlement remains have been found for about 1,500 years after the early Old Kingdom. Flooding or a shift in a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> may explain this, and the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadjet">Wadjet</a> seems to have stayed in use. Buto reappears in texts of the 18th Dynasty and was resettled from the late 8th century BCE, with high activity under the Saite kings of the 26th Dynasty. In Ptolemaic times it was the capital of the Phthenotes nome. Greek writers praised its temple and the oracle of Wadjet. Habitation most likely ended around the 6th or 7th century CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1886 W. M. Flinders Petrie suggested that the two mounds were the twin cities Pe and Dep. The Egypt Exploration Society dug at Buto from 1964 to 1969, and the German Archaeological Institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> has worked there since the early 1980s. Since 2018 Kafrelsheikh University has used satellite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radar">radar</a> and electrical resistivity tomography to locate buried remains, and in 2021 its excavations on Kom C recovered terracotta figurines and amulets of deities, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadjet">Wadjet</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> child.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Buto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Later Egyptian tradition held that before unification the Delta formed a kingdom of its own, with its capital at <b>Buto</b> (Pe and Dep) and the red crown as its emblem.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lower-egypt">Kingdom of Lower Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
